WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.118
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.118 --> 00:00:10.763
My dad grew up in a small town in
Eastern India named Hazaribagh,

00:00:10.763 --> 00:00:13.803
where life revolved around coal.

00:00:13.803 --> 00:00:16.461
Schools, bridges, hospitals,

00:00:16.461 --> 00:00:21.969
libraries were built over generations
directly by the coal industry.

00:00:21.969 --> 00:00:26.364
Coal companies even provided
rights to movie theaters.

00:00:26.364 --> 00:00:31.373
My grandfather worked here for
30 years as a coal mining engineer.

00:00:31.373 --> 00:00:35.321
When it was time for
my dad to decide on his career,

00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:40.129
there were now two options in
front of him, coal and now oil.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:44.980
He picked the latter partly because
their offices had air conditioning.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:50.619
If you've ever been to India over the
summer, you may understand that choice.

00:00:50.619 --> 00:00:55.170
As a result of that simple decision,
my family's life transformed.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.236
We moved from India to China to Texas.

00:00:59.236 --> 00:01:03.254
All the while, I was privileged
enough to receive an elite education.

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:08.060
But despite that, when it was time for
me to decide on my career,

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.184
I also felt drawn towards energy issues.

00:01:11.184 --> 00:01:15.450
How predictable and boring?

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.299
I wondered if I should distance myself
away from energy just to avoid doing what

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:23.249
two generations in my
family had already done.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.111
Ultimately, I settled on clean energy,
telling myself,

00:01:27.111 --> 00:01:30.665
this is different enough from
what my parents did, and

00:01:30.665 --> 00:01:34.390
maybe I can still retain some
semblance of originality.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.145
And I enjoyed it too, for
the first time in my career,

00:01:38.145 --> 00:01:43.417
it felt as though I was connected to
a mission that was broader than myself.

00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:47.559
But deep down,
something still didn't feel quite right.

00:01:47.559 --> 00:01:52.004
As clean energy transition and
sustainability became mainstream

00:01:52.004 --> 00:01:57.555
buzzwords, it seemed as though everyone
on my LinkedIn feed, including myself,

00:01:57.555 --> 00:02:02.339
had suddenly taken up arms in some
sort of war of biblical proportions.

00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:06.348
There was an epic battle being
waged against climate change and

00:02:06.348 --> 00:02:11.049
every investment banker with a green
bond was a climate justice warrior.

00:02:11.049 --> 00:02:16.881
Now I'm all for enthusiasm, but it
started to feel a little too convenient,

00:02:16.881 --> 00:02:20.935
a little inauthentic and
frankly, a little hollow.

00:02:20.935 --> 00:02:23.893
Personally, I felt ungrounded.

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:28.999
The more I tethered myself to this
grand ambition of climate change,

00:02:28.999 --> 00:02:33.235
the more distant I felt from my roots and
family history.

00:02:33.235 --> 00:02:37.418
But as I was working to
reconcile this personal dilemma,

00:02:37.418 --> 00:02:41.613
I came across a broader and
much more important problem.

00:02:41.613 --> 00:02:47.106
Amidst all the excitement about new,
clean energy technologies and innovations,

00:02:47.106 --> 00:02:51.919
what was often never talked about were
the people actually on the ground.

00:02:51.919 --> 00:02:57.079
I wondered, as the world moved away
from fossil fuels, what would happen

00:02:57.079 --> 00:03:02.504
to communities like my dad's hometown
who depended on coal for everything?

00:03:02.504 --> 00:03:07.120
Who is looking out for them?

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.909
Let's take a moment to consider the life
of an average coal mine worker in

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:13.489
a developing economy.

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:16.196
They have worked in the mines for
20 years,

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:20.913
they've developed hearing impairments
from being too close to blast sites.

00:03:20.913 --> 00:03:26.910
They have respiratory illnesses from
direct exposure to coal ash day in and

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:27.679
day out.

00:03:27.679 --> 00:03:32.433
But despite these extreme challenges,
this job is a blessing,

00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:38.344
it is virtually the only thing that
separates his family from extreme poverty.

00:03:38.344 --> 00:03:44.169
Now imagine what happens when nearby coal
plants in the surrounding area are shut.

00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:48.447
He loses his job,
there's no other industry in town,

00:03:48.447 --> 00:03:52.818
which means that the only two
options are to leave town or

00:03:52.818 --> 00:03:55.704
try his hand as a daily wage laborer.

00:03:55.704 --> 00:04:02.028
How tragic that the same person who
bore the brunt of all the environmental

00:04:02.028 --> 00:04:08.562
harms of coal will now also bear the brunt
of the transition to clean energy.

00:04:08.562 --> 00:04:11.262
As countries move away from fossil fuels,

00:04:11.262 --> 00:04:15.919
millions in already vulnerable
communities will be put at serious risk.

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:20.429
Over 10 million people are dependent
on coal just in India, but

00:04:20.429 --> 00:04:26.179
this is a problem for the world, China,
Indonesia, Australia, West Virginia.

00:04:26.179 --> 00:04:29.654
All these places have
communities that are dependent.

00:04:29.654 --> 00:04:33.544
But let me be clear, coal does need to go.

00:04:33.544 --> 00:04:37.555
If the world has to come close to
meeting our climate change objectives,

00:04:37.555 --> 00:04:40.989
we do need to phase out fossil
fuels over the next few decades.

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:46.286
But the way in which we manage this
transition could be the difference

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:50.409
between sustainable growth and
human catastrophe.

00:04:50.409 --> 00:04:55.002
Now there are some who say that all
transitions have winners and losers,

00:04:55.002 --> 00:04:56.891
it's been this way forever.

00:04:56.891 --> 00:04:58.624
That might be true.

00:04:58.624 --> 00:05:01.884
But the sheer magnitude
of the current energy

00:05:01.884 --> 00:05:06.449
transition will be disruptive
like nothing we have ever seen.

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:11.264
And when lives are disrupted,
especially in developing economies,

00:05:11.264 --> 00:05:15.598
the result is not just unemployment,
it's extreme poverty.

00:05:15.598 --> 00:05:20.364
Now, I've also heard the argument
that there'll be new clean energy

00:05:20.364 --> 00:05:24.319
jobs to make up for
the jobs that are lost in fossil fuels and

00:05:24.319 --> 00:05:29.271
maybe even more jobs will be created
in the aggregate over the long term.

00:05:29.271 --> 00:05:34.093
But human beings don't live in
the aggregate or over the long term.

00:05:34.093 --> 00:05:37.479
They live in the here and now,
with all the struggles and

00:05:37.479 --> 00:05:41.028
dependencies that have been
deepened over generations.

00:05:41.028 --> 00:05:44.994
Some also say that it should be the
government's responsibility to take care

00:05:44.994 --> 00:05:49.142
of those who are left behind so that the
private sector can focus on innovation and

00:05:49.142 --> 00:05:49.884
technology.

00:05:49.884 --> 00:05:54.152
But that's simply too convenient,
just relying on the government to

00:05:54.152 --> 00:05:59.090
take care of social consequences is not
a strategy, it's a feel good exercise.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:03.853
We need to move beyond wishful
thinking and think holistically about

00:06:03.853 --> 00:06:08.297
how our energy transition cannot
just be clean, but also just.

00:06:08.297 --> 00:06:11.594
Now, no two communities will
have the same solution.

00:06:11.594 --> 00:06:16.665
For some, it could be a direct swap of
fossil jobs to clean energy jobs, for

00:06:16.665 --> 00:06:21.739
many others, like my dad's hometown,
entirely new industries have to be

00:06:21.739 --> 00:06:26.759
created, whether it's eco-tourism,
agriculture or construction.

00:06:26.759 --> 00:06:31.647
But here's the deal,
investing in the communities that will

00:06:31.647 --> 00:06:35.972
be left behind is not just ethical,
it's practical.

00:06:35.972 --> 00:06:40.240
These same communities represent
much of the opposition to clean

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.109
energy legislation today.

00:06:42.109 --> 00:06:45.587
So investing in them, supporting them, and

00:06:45.587 --> 00:06:50.253
making sure they're not left
behind actually maximizes our

00:06:50.253 --> 00:06:54.659
chances of achieving
the transition in the first place.

00:06:54.659 --> 00:06:57.350
Now, there's a lot of work
that's left to be done, but

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.831
there are some important
early signs of progress.

00:06:59.831 --> 00:07:02.625
For example,
the Just Transition Initiative and

00:07:02.625 --> 00:07:07.148
the Climate Justice Alliance works with
companies all around the world to pursue

00:07:07.148 --> 00:07:11.274
climate change mitigation but
also support marginalized communities,

00:07:11.274 --> 00:07:13.489
all the way from South Africa to India.

00:07:13.489 --> 00:07:18.616
But if we want to make a real dent in the
problem, our efforts have to be bolder and

00:07:18.616 --> 00:07:23.608
broader, this is about a lot more than
just coal or any other energy source.

00:07:23.608 --> 00:07:28.384
This is a while about how we think of
sustainability in the first place.

00:07:28.384 --> 00:07:33.005
Sustainability must be broader than
just protecting the environment.

00:07:33.005 --> 00:07:39.127
It must include protecting people,
especially those who are most at risk.

00:07:39.127 --> 00:07:42.911
Now, companies and
governments are certainly important, but

00:07:42.911 --> 00:07:46.489
I believe that you and
I have the most important role to play.

00:07:46.489 --> 00:07:50.738
We know that Millennials and
Gen Ziers are the most likely to make

00:07:50.738 --> 00:07:55.156
purchasing decisions based on
sustainable business practices.

00:07:55.156 --> 00:07:58.751
So your pocketbook can
actually be a vote and

00:07:58.751 --> 00:08:04.492
expression of solidarity with the voices
who are not yet at the table.

00:08:04.492 --> 00:08:08.315
And as future business leaders,
it's our opportunity and

00:08:08.315 --> 00:08:13.449
responsibility to ensure that those same
voices also belong in the boardroom.

00:08:13.449 --> 00:08:17.506
So the next time you're stepping out for
your morning coffee, or

00:08:17.506 --> 00:08:21.268
deciding what stocks to back on
Robin Hood, or preparing for

00:08:21.268 --> 00:08:25.954
the next meeting in the boardroom,
push yourself to take that extra step.

00:08:25.954 --> 00:08:29.954
Challenge yourself to do
a little extra research.

00:08:29.954 --> 00:08:35.805
Ask yourself, how can I support
a world that is not just more clean,

00:08:35.805 --> 00:08:37.149
but more just?

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:40.660
As we scramble to reduce
our carbon footprint,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:44.099
what can I do for
those who will be left behind?

00:08:44.099 --> 00:08:45.184
Thank you.

00:08:45.184 --> 00:08:46.339
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:46.339 --> 00:08:51.179
[MUSIC]

